2007_AMC_10B_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"triangle-area-circumradius","label":"Use triangle area and circumradius","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triangle-area-circumradius":["cnm_p148","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p148","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 59/4","reason":"Let the legs be a and b and hypotenuse c. Then ab = 40 and a + b + c = 32. Since (a + b)^2 - c^2 = 2ab = 80, a + b - c = 80/32 = 5/2. Subtracting gives 2c = 32 - 5/2, so c = 59/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_10A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"right-triangle-inradius","label":"Use the inradius","methodDirectNodeIds":{"right-triangle-inradius":["math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_264","cnm_263"]},"nodeIds":["math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_264","cnm_263"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, (3/2)(√2 − 1)","reason":"The roots are z = −6 + √3 exp(ikπ/4), so the circumradius is √3. The five unordered cyclic-gap types summing to 8 give areas (3/2)(√2 − 1), 3/2, 3√2/2, 3, and (3/2)(1 + √2). The first is smallest and is attained by three consecutive vertices.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"translated-octagon-coordinates","label":"Translate the roots and use coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"translated-octagon-coordinates":["math:roots-of-unity","math:extremal-principle","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_084"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:extremal-principle","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_084"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Independent native mathematical review confirms inconsistent geometric givens. Second-pass targeted model independently derived the same contradiction after exposure to the candidate limitation; this is not blind diverse evidence.","derivedAnswer":"No such triangle exists; 27 is only the formal substitution result.","reason":"The intersection of the internal angle bisectors is the incenter. Its distances to all sides equal r=3; s=18/2=9. Area partition would give K=rs=27. But Heron gives K²=s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c); the three positive factors s-a,s-b,s-c sum to s, so AM-GM gives K²≤s(s/3)³ and K≤s²/(3√3)=9√3<27. The displayed data are inconsistent, independently of the stored answer.","recordedAnswer":"27","status":"confirmed_conflict"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The keyed value 27 is the intended formal calculation, but a triangle with perimeter 18 cannot have inradius 3.","confirmed_inconsistent_geometric_data","formal_answer_not_geometrically_attainable"],"questionId":"cnm_128_q5","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"formal-inradius-formula","label":"Use area equals inradius times semiperimeter","methodDirectNodeIds":{"formal-inradius-formula":["cnm_264","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_264","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 13 units","reason":"The sides are 14, 6+t, and 8+t, with semiperimeter 14+t. Since K=4(14+t), Heron's formula gives 16(14+t)^2=48t(14+t), so t=7. The sides are 13, 14, and 15.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1953_AMC_12_Q50","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"heron-and-inradius","label":"Combine Heron's formula with K=rs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"heron-and-inradius":["math:heron-formula","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["math:heron-formula","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_042"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_262","cnm_p148","cnm_111"],"id":"tangent-lengths-and-area","label":"Compute the area in two ways","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-lengths-and-area":["cnm_263","cnm_264","math:heron-formula","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_263","cnm_264","math:heron-formula","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_042","cnm_262","cnm_p148","cnm_111"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. x=hd/(2h+d)","reason":"Pythagoras gives (h+d-x)^2=(h+x)^2+d^2, which reduces to hd=x(2h+d). Thus x=hd/(2h+d). For positive h and d, this gives x>0 and MA=h+d-x>0, so the squaring is valid.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1954_AMC_12_Q35","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"incircle-area","label":"Identify x as the inradius","methodDirectNodeIds":{"incircle-area":["cnm_264","cnm_263","cnm_p148","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_264","cnm_263","cnm_p148","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, s²/6","reason":"The triangle's inradius is r=s√3/6. The square's diagonal is 2r, so its area is (2r)²/2=2r²=s²/6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1967_AMC_12_Q03","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"triangle-area-inradius","label":"Use area equals inradius times semiperimeter","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triangle-area-inradius":["math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_174","cnm_191"]},"nodeIds":["math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_174","cnm_191"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_264","cnm_191","cnm_p148"],"id":"inradius-from-triangle-areas","label":"Method 1: Compare triangle areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"inradius-from-triangle-areas":["cnm_266","cnm_p089","cnm_174","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_266","cnm_p089","cnm_174","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_264","cnm_191","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 5√17/16","reason":"Place the bottom extreme corners at (±1,0) and the top corners at (±1/2,2). For any center (x,t), R² is at least max{1+t²,1/4+(2-t)²}. This bound is minimized where the two expressions agree, at t=13/16, giving R²=425/256. Center (0,13/16) attains this bound and contains all other vertices, so R=5√17/16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1972_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"supporting-triangle","label":"Acute-triangle circumradius","methodDirectNodeIds":{"supporting-triangle":["cnm_265","cnm_174","cnm_p148","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_265","cnm_174","cnm_p148","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 30","reason":"Let I be the incenter. Since MN is parallel to BC, the angle bisectors give BM = MI and CN = NI. Thus the perimeter is AM + AN + MN = (AB − BM) + (AC − CN) + MI + IN = AB + AC = 30.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12A_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"inradius-altitude-similarity","label":"Use inradius and similarity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"inradius-altitude-similarity":["math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_p148","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_p148","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_112"],"id":"compare-inradius-and-altitude","label":"Compare the inradius and altitude","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-inradius-and-altitude":["cnm_264","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_p148","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_264","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_p148","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_069","cnm_112"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 195/8","reason":"Let O be the circumcenter of ABC. Since OD⊥AB and OE⊥BC, the points B,D,E,O lie on the circle with diameter BO. Similarly C,E,F,O are concyclic, so X=O. Heron's formula gives area √(21·8·7·6)=84. Thus R=(13·14·15)/(4·84)=65/8, and XA+XB+XC=3R=195/8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"perpendicular-bisector-circles","label":"Identify the intersection as the circumcenter","nodeIds":["cnm_133","cnm_254","math:heron-formula","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_174","cnm_250"],"id":"solution:perpendicular-bisector-circles:cdc75e126707","label":"Recognize the circumcenter","methodDirectNodeIds":{"perpendicular-bisector-circles":["cnm_265","cnm_133","cnm_254","math:heron-formula","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_265","cnm_133","cnm_254","math:heron-formula","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_055","cnm_174","cnm_250"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 3(10 - √2)/28","reason":"The angle-bisector theorem gives AD = 15/7 and BD = 20/7. The equal distances from D to the two legs are 12/7, so CD = 12√2/7. Using r = area/semiperimeter gives r_a = 3/(3 + √2) and r_b = 4/(4 + √2). Their ratio simplifies to 3(10 - √2)/28.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"subtriangle-area-semiperimeter","label":"Use subtriangle areas and semiperimeters","methodDirectNodeIds":{"subtriangle-area-semiperimeter":["cnm_128","math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_p148","cnm_174","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_128","math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_p148","cnm_174","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_065","cnm_114","cnm_171"],"id":"areas-and-semiperimeters","label":"Method 1: Use areas and semiperimeters","methodDirectNodeIds":{"areas-and-semiperimeters":["cnm_264","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_128","cnm_p148","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_264","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_128","cnm_p148","cnm_174","cnm_065","cnm_114","cnm_171"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 15","reason":"The semiperimeter is 39. Heron's formula gives area sqrt(39*12*13*14)=78sqrt(14), so the inradius is 2sqrt(14). Also cos B=(27^2+25^2-26^2)/(2*27*25)=113/225, hence sin(B/2)=2sqrt(14)/15. Therefore BI=r/sin(B/2)=15.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_12A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"inradius-and-half-angle","label":"Use the inradius and a half-angle derived from an isosceles cosine-law triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"inradius-and-half-angle":["math:general-cosine-law-application","math:heron-formula","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_137","cnm_276"]},"nodeIds":["math:general-cosine-law-application","math:heron-formula","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_137","cnm_276"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p148","cnm_262"],"id":"incircle-area-and-tangents","label":"Method 2: Use the incircle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"incircle-area-and-tangents":["cnm_114","cnm_264","cnm_263","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_084","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_114","cnm_264","cnm_263","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_084","cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_262"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 7/2","reason":"Set C=(0,0), A=(12,0), and B=(0,5). Then O=(6,5/2), I=(2,2), and R=13/2. Write M=(r,r), with 0<r≤5 because tangency is to the leg segments. Internal tangency gives (r−6)^2+(r−5/2)^2=(13/2−r)^2, yielding r=4; external tangency would give r=30, which is inadmissible. In the bounding rectangle [2,6]×[2,4], subtracting the three corner triangles gives [MOI]=8−2−3/2−1=7/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"coordinate-circle-tangency","label":"Locate the centers using coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-circle-tangency":["cnm_264","cnm_265","cnm_h181","cnm_h173","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","math:shoelace-formula"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_264","cnm_265","cnm_h181","cnm_h173","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","math:shoelace-formula"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 26π","reason":"Let O be the given circle's center. Since OB is perpendicular to AB, AO^2 = AB^2 + OB^2 = 54 + 50 = 104. Both B and C lie on the circle with diameter AO, so the required circle has radius squared 104/4 = 26 and area 26π.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_10A_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"similarity-and-area-radius","label":"Similarity and triangle area-radius formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similarity-and-area-radius":["cnm_133","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_p148","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_133","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_p148","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 3√3:2","reason":"For circle radius r, the equilateral triangle has side 2√3r and height 3r, so its area is 3√3r^2. The square has diagonal 2r and area 2r^2. Their area ratio is 3√3:2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1963_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_264","cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_p089","cnm_191"],"id":"triangle-area-decomposition","label":"Express both areas using the circle radius","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triangle-area-decomposition":["cnm_266","math:triangle-area-radius-relations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_266","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_137","cnm_264","cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_p089","cnm_191"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 10√5−20 meters","reason":"Put the sphere's ground contact at (0,0), its center at (0,r), and the shadow tip at (10,0). The tangent ray has equation x+2y−10=0. Thus (10−2r)/√5=r, giving r=10/(2+√5)=10√5−20.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1983_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_260","cnm_p148","cnm_174"],"id":"reflect-ray-and-sum-areas","label":"Reflect the ray and compare areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reflect-ray-and-sum-areas":["cnm_281","cnm_262","cnm_132","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_171"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_281","cnm_262","cnm_132","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_171","cnm_260","cnm_p148","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 2√6","reason":"The semiperimeter is 21. A convex quadrilateral containing a radius-r disk has area K≥21r, while the fixed-side area bound is K≤√(7·12·14·9)=42√6. Equality is attainable by a cyclic tangential quadrilateral with successive vertex tangent lengths 8,6,3,4, for which r²=8·3=24. Concave candidates lie inside hull triangles with smaller possible disk radii. Hence the maximum is 2√6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"maximum-area-bound","label":"Bound the area by cosine laws and construct a tangential extremizer","methodDirectNodeIds":{"maximum-area-bound":["math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_p148","math:cauchy-schwarz","cnm_h062","cnm_h064","cnm_276","math:heron-formula","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_262"]},"nodeIds":["math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_p148","math:cauchy-schwarz","cnm_h062","cnm_h064","cnm_276","math:heron-formula","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_262"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:triangle-area-radius-relations","status":"mapped","total":37}
